lder adults. These drugs are highly anticholinergic, and their clearance is reduced in the elderly. They cross the blood-brain barrier readily, leading to severe central nervous system effects (sedation, cognitive impairment, delirium, falls) and peripheral effects (urinary retention, dry mouth, constipation, pupillary dilation). For insomnia or allergic rhinitis, safer alternatives such as non-pharmacological sleep hygiene, low-dose melatonin, second-generation antihistamines (e.g., cetirizine, fexofenadine), or intranasal steroid sprays should be utilized.'),
