    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-old woman is evaluated in the clinic for lower back pain after lifting a grocery bag. She has a history of benign prostatic hyperplasia and mild cognitive impairment. The patient asks for a prescription of **Cyclobenzaprine 10 mg** three times daily, which she heard is effective for muscle spasms. Which of the following is the Beers Criteria recommendation regarding skeletal muscle relaxants in older adults?', '["Only Carisoprodol should be avoided; all other skeletal muscle relaxants have no anticholinergic properties and can be used without restrictions", "Skeletal muscle relaxants are safe to use in older adults provided they are combined with a long-acting benzodiazepine to maximize muscle relaxation", "Skeletal muscle relaxants (such as Cyclobenzaprine) are highly recommended as first-line therapy for acute musculoskeletal pain in patients over 75", "Cyclobenzaprine is the drug of choice for elderly patients with BPH because it relaxes the bladder neck and improves urinary flow", "Avoid all skeletal muscle relaxants in older adults, as they are poorly tolerated due to strong anticholinergic side effects, sedation, and a high risk of falls, fractures, and delirium, while having questionable efficacy"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Beers Criteria recommends avoiding all skeletal muscle relaxants** (such as **Cyclobenzaprine**, carisoprodol, chlorzoxazone, metaxalone, methocarbamol, orphenadrine) in older adults. Most skeletal muscle relaxants are **highly anticholinergic**, sedating, and cause significant central nervous system depression. Consequently, they are poorly tolerated in older adults and are associated with a **high risk of falls, fractures, cognitive impairment, delirium, and urinary retention** (which can cause acute urinary obstruction in patients with baseline BPH). Furthermore, clinical evidence of their effectiveness at doses tolerated by older adults is weak. Acute musculoskeletal pain in older adults should be managed with non-pharmacological therapies (heat, physical therapy), topical NSAIDs, acetaminophen, or short courses of oral NSAIDs (if no contraindications exist and with gastroprotection).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-old male is seen for a routine wellness visit. Medication review shows the patient has been taking **Omeprazole 40 mg daily for 6 years** for ''''acid reflux.'''' He reports no active reflux symptoms and has never had a documented history of peptic ulcer disease, Barrett''''s esophagus, or severe erosive esophagitis. Which of the following represents the Beers Criteria guidance regarding the long-term use of Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs) in older adults, and what are the associated risks?', '["PPIs should be continued indefinitely in all older adults as they are completely safe and prevent all-cause gastrointestinal bleeding", "Long-term PPI use is highly recommended by the Beers Criteria because it enhances calcium and magnesium absorption in the duodenum", "PPIs should be stopped immediately without tapering, as there is no risk of rebound acid hypersecretion", "Avoid PPIs only in patients with a history of hypertension; for all other patients, they should be used first-line for mild dyspepsia", "Avoid scheduled PPI use for >8 weeks unless high-risk indications are present (e.g., oral corticosteroid/chronic NSAID use, severe esophagitis); long-term use increases risks of C. difficile infection, bone loss/fractures, and B12 deficiency"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Beers Criteria recommends avoiding scheduled Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)** use for **more than 8 weeks** in older adults, unless the patient has a clear, high-risk indication (e.g., chronic oral corticosteroid or NSAID use, severe erosive esophagitis, documented Barrett''''s esophagus, or pathological hypersecretory conditions). Long-term PPI use is associated with several significant risks in older adults: (1) **Clostridioides difficile-associated diarrhea and pneumonia** (due to suppression of gastric acid, which normally acts as a barrier against pathogens), (2) **bone loss, osteoporosis, and hip/wrist fractures** (due to impaired absorption of calcium and magnesium, which require an acidic environment), and (3) **Vitamin B12 deficiency** (as gastric acid is required to cleave B12 from dietary protein). When de-prescribing PPIs, a **gradual taper** or switching to an as-needed H2 receptor antagonist is recommended to avoid **rebound gastric acid hypersecretion**.');
